{
 "slug": "expanding-and-factorising-quadratics",
 "title": "Expanding and Factorising Quadratics",
 "seo_title": "Year 9 Quadratics Worksheets: Expanding Double Brackets, Factorising x² + bx + c and Difference of Two Squares",
 "short_title": "Quadratics",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS3: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions to maintain equivalence by: expanding products of two or more binomials; factorising quadratic expressions of the form x² + bx + c, including the difference of two squares.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 9 quadratics worksheets: expand double brackets and squares, the difference of two squares, factorise x² + bx + c with positive and negative numbers, and solve by factorising. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "To factorise x² + bx + c, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.",
 "key_sub": "(x + a)² = x² + 2ax + a², and x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>To <b>expand</b> two brackets, multiply each term in the first by each term in the second, then collect the middle terms: (<i>x</i> + <i>a</i>)(<i>x</i> + <i>b</i>) = <i>x</i>² + (<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>)<i>x</i> + <i>ab</i>.</p><p>To <b>factorise</b> <i>x</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>, find two numbers that <b>multiply to <i>c</i></b> and <b>add to <i>b</i></b>. The <b>difference of two squares</b>: <i>x</i>² − <i>a</i>² = (<i>x</i> + <i>a</i>)(<i>x</i> − <i>a</i>).</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand (<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> − 7).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i>² − 7<i>x</i> + 3<i>x</i> − 21.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i>² − 4<i>x</i> − 21</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factorise <i>x</i>² + 2<i>x</i> − 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply to −15, add to 2: 5 and −3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (<i>x</i> + 5)(<i>x</i> − 3)</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve <i>x</i>² − 5<i>x</i> + 6 = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> − 3) = 0.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 2 or <i>x</i> = 3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Missing the middle terms: (<i>x</i> + 3)² is <i>x</i>² + 6<i>x</i> + 9, not <i>x</i>² + 9.</li><li>Sign errors: (−3) × (−7) = +21, but (−3) × 7 = −21.</li><li>Finding numbers that multiply to <i>c</i> but do not add to <i>b</i>.</li><li>Giving only the positive solution of <i>x</i>² = 49. There are two: 7 and −7.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do I factorise a quadratic?",
   "a": "For x² + bx + c, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. If they are p and q, the answer is (x + p)(x + q)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the difference of two squares?",
   "a": "x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). For example x² − 49 = (x + 7)(x − 7)."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is (x + 3)² not x² + 9?",
   "a": "Because (x + 3)² = (x + 3)(x + 3), which has two middle terms of 3x. So it is x² + 6x + 9."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I solve a quadratic by factorising?",
   "a": "Factorise so the equation is (x − p)(x − q) = 0. One of the brackets must be zero, so x = p or x = q."
  }
 ],
